System Requirements:

Ubuntu 20.04 or later / CentOS 8 or later
Minimum 2GB RAM
10GB available disk space
Internet connectivity for package installation
Task 1: Install PostgreSQL
Step 1.1: Update System Packages
# For Ubuntu/Debian
sudo apt update && sudo apt upgrade -y

# For CentOS/RHEL
sudo dnf update -y
Step 1.2: Install PostgreSQL
# For Ubuntu/Debian (installs PostgreSQL 14+)
sudo apt install postgresql postgresql-contrib -y

# For CentOS/RHEL
sudo dnf install postgresql-server postgresql-contrib -y
sudo postgresql-setup --initdb
Step 1.3: Start and Enable PostgreSQL Service
# Start the service
sudo systemctl start postgresql

# Enable service to start on boot
sudo systemctl enable postgresql

# Verify service status
sudo systemctl status postgresql
Expected Output: Service should show "active (running)" status.

Step 1.4: Verify Installation
# Check PostgreSQL version
psql --version

# Check listening ports
sudo ss -tunelp | grep 5432
Note: By default, PostgreSQL listens on localhost (127.0.0.1) port 5432.

Task 2: Configure Users and Roles
Step 2.1: Access PostgreSQL as Default User
# Switch to postgres system user
sudo -i -u postgres

# Access PostgreSQL prompt
psql
You should see the PostgreSQL prompt: postgres=#

Step 2.2: Create Database Roles
Execute the following SQL commands in the PostgreSQL prompt:

-- Create an admin role with login capability
CREATE ROLE db_admin WITH LOGIN PASSWORD 'SecureAdminPass123!';

-- Grant superuser privileges
ALTER ROLE db_admin WITH SUPERUSER CREATEDB CREATEROLE;

-- Create a regular application user
CREATE ROLE app_user WITH LOGIN PASSWORD 'AppUserPass456!';

-- Create a read-only role
CREATE ROLE readonly_user WITH LOGIN PASSWORD 'ReadOnlyPass789!';

-- Verify created roles
\du
Step 2.3: Create a Test Database and Grant Permissions
-- Create a test database
CREATE DATABASE testdb OWNER app_user;

-- Connect to the database
\c testdb

-- Create a sample table
CREATE TABLE products (
    id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY,
    name VARCHAR(100) NOT NULL,
    price DECIMAL(10,2),
    created_at TIMESTAMP DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP
);

-- Grant privileges to app_user
GRANT ALL PRIVILEGES ON DATABASE testdb TO app_user;
GRANT ALL PRIVILEGES ON ALL TABLES IN SCHEMA public TO app_user;
GRANT USAGE, SELECT ON ALL SEQUENCES IN SCHEMA public TO app_user;

-- Grant read-only access to readonly_user
GRANT CONNECT ON DATABASE testdb TO readonly_user;
GRANT USAGE ON SCHEMA public TO readonly_user;
GRANT SELECT ON ALL TABLES IN SCHEMA public TO readonly_user;

-- Exit psql
\q
Exit from postgres user:

exit
Task 3: Configure Authentication
Step 3.1: Locate Configuration Files
# Find PostgreSQL configuration directory
sudo -u postgres psql -c "SHOW config_file;"

# Typical locations:
# Ubuntu/Debian: /etc/postgresql/[version]/main/
# CentOS/RHEL: /var/lib/pgsql/data/
Step 3.2: Configure pg_hba.conf for Authentication
# Backup the original file
sudo cp /etc/postgresql/*/main/pg_hba.conf /etc/postgresql/*/main/pg_hba.conf.backup

# Edit pg_hba.conf (adjust path for your system)
sudo nano /etc/postgresql/*/main/pg_hba.conf
Add/Modify these lines (place before the default entries):

# TYPE  DATABASE        USER            ADDRESS                 METHOD

# Local connections use md5 password authentication
local   all             all                                     md5

# IPv4 local connections
host    all             all             127.0.0.1/32            md5

# IPv6 local connections
host    all             all             ::1/128                 md5

# Allow connections from local network (optional - adjust subnet)
host    testdb          app_user        192.168.1.0/24          md5
host    testdb          readonly_user   192.168.1.0/24          md5
Authentication Methods Explained:

md5: Password authentication (encrypted)
trust: No password required (use only for local development)
peer: Use operating system username
scram-sha-256: More secure password authentication (PostgreSQL 10+)
Step 3.3: Configure postgresql.conf for Network Access
# Edit postgresql.conf
sudo nano /etc/postgresql/*/main/postgresql.conf
Find and modify these settings:

# Listen on all interfaces (for production, specify exact IP)
listen_addresses = 'localhost'  # Change to '*' for all interfaces or specific IP

# Maximum connections
max_connections = 100

# Shared buffers (25% of RAM recommended)
shared_buffers = 256MB

# Logging configuration
logging_collector = on
log_directory = 'log'
log_filename = 'postgresql-%Y-%m-%d_%H%M%S.log'
log_statement = 'all'  # Change to 'ddl' or 'mod' in production
Step 3.4: Restart PostgreSQL to Apply Changes
sudo systemctl restart postgresql

# Verify service restarted successfully
sudo systemctl status postgresql
Task 4: Test Connectivity
Step 4.1: Test Local Connection with Password Authentication
# Test connection as app_user
psql -U app_user -d testdb -h localhost

